~ A live HTTP GET with the `core.http` client: a `Request` carries the method and the URL,
~ `send` puts it on the wire over core.net's `@tcpRequest` and parses the reply into
~ `Ok(Response)`. On success the program exits 0.
~ NOTE: this makes a LIVE network call to example.com:80 when run.
<< core.http

^ = () -> $ => <
  result = Request { method = Get, url = "http://example.com/" }.send()
  assert(result, isOk())
  response = result ?
    | Ok(page) => page
    | NotOk(_) => Response { raw = "" }
  assert(response.status(), equals(200))

  ~ The page's text changes, so assert it arrived rather than what it says.
  assert(response.body().size > 0, equals(true))

  ~ The reply carries headers, read case-insensitively off the live response.
  assert(response.header("Content-Type"), isOk())

  ~ A transport failure comes back as NotOk, not a crash.
  assert(Request { method = Get, url = "http://127.0.0.1:1/" }.send(), isNotOk())
>
